<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      The app's first network access: validating the token against api.github.com, and from
      iteration 2 on, checking for a release. Cleartext is not permitted by default at this
      targetSdk and nothing here re-enables it.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
      Required to hand a downloaded .apk to Android's installer. It permits *asking*: the owner still
      confirms on Android's own screen, and they must also have allowed this app under
      "Install unknown apps" - which UpdateInstaller.canInstall checks before offering.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES" />

    <!--
      The first permission this application has ever asked a human for, and the only one it asks for
      at all - INTERNET and REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES are granted at install with nothing to refuse.

      It is here because a live viewfinder reads frames in OUR process. The route it replaces needed
      no permission precisely because the capture happened inside the system camera app, and that
      route did not work: on 2026-08-09 the owner could not pair by camera at all, and the pairing was
      carried by the file route. REQ-0019 argues the trade rather than assuming it.

      What this costs, stated: a dialog the owner sees, can refuse, and can revoke later. So refusal
      is a supported state and not an error - see CameraAccess - and choosing a FILE never triggers
      the request at all. The file route is the only one that has ever completed a pairing here.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />

    <!--
      The car - REQ-0044. The terminal is drawn on the rectangle Android Auto lends to navigation
      apps, and the host lends it only to an app holding these three. They are the car library's own
      permissions, granted at install with nothing to refuse, and they are the reason the app declares
      the navigation category: nothing else on Android Auto lets an app draw its own pixels.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="androidx.car.app.NAVIGATION_TEMPLATES" />
    <uses-permission android:name="androidx.car.app.MAP_TEMPLATES" />
    <!--
      The third one, and the one the navigation guide never names. Found on the head unit on
      2026-09-05: with only the two above, the host refuses setSurfaceCallback with "The car app does
      not have a required permission: androidx.car.app.ACCESS_SURFACE", and the refusal arrives as an
      exception that kills the whole app - phone screen included.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="androidx.car.app.ACCESS_SURFACE" />

    <!--
      The second permission this app asks a person for. The car's microphone button runs the phone's
      own speech recogniser, which records in OUR process; the car library has no speech of its own.
      Asked for when the button is pressed and not before, and refusing it leaves the keys and the
      car's keyboard working - see CarVoice. Where the audio comes from while projecting is REQ-0044
      §5, question 3, which only a car can answer.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />

    <!--
      required="false", and this line is load-bearing rather than tidy.

      Declaring CAMERA implies <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera"> with
      required="true", which asks app stores to hide the app from every device without a camera. This
      app is a terminal client whose other route needs no camera at all, so a phone without one must
      still be able to install it and pair by file. Stated explicitly because the default is a filter
      nobody chose and nobody would see.
    -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera" android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera.autofocus" android:required="false" />

    <!--
        No USE_BIOMETRIC. It was here to raise the system unlock prompt when the phone's own key had
        gone to sleep; the key is no longer bound to a recent unlock (2026-07-29), so there is no
        prompt to raise and the permission would be one this app does not use. Removing it narrows
        what the app asks for, which is the direction this manifest only ever moves in.
    -->

    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.BewareOfSugar">
        <!--
          adjustResize, stated rather than left to the system.

          The default is SOFT_INPUT_ADJUST_UNSPECIFIED, which lets Android choose between resizing
          the window and panning it, per screen, by inspecting the content. That is why the terminal
          screen had TWO layouts with the same keyboard: sometimes the window shrank and sometimes it
          slid, and the choice was not ours. A layout that is right on average is the same defect as
          one that is wrong.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.BewareOfSugar">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />

                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          The car - REQ-0044. The Android Auto host binds this service and asks it for templates; the
          descriptor tells the host this is a template app, and the api level is the lowest the library
          supports, so no car is refused on that count.

          Exported, because the host is another process - and that is why TerminalCarAppService
          validates the host against the library's allowlist of Google's signatures rather than
          admitting any caller. An app on the phone that could bind this would read the terminal and
          type into it. CarManifestTest reads this file.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.android.gms.car.application"
            android:resource="@xml/automotive_app_desc" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="androidx.car.app.minCarApiLevel"
            android:value="1" />
        <service
            android:name=".car.TerminalCarAppService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/car_app_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="androidx.car.app.CarAppService" />
                <category android:name="androidx.car.app.category.NAVIGATION" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!--
          Lets the installer read exactly one downloaded .apk, for exactly one intent. Not exported,
          so no other app can enumerate or read what is in here. The authority is duplicated in
          UpdateInstaller.AUTHORITY, which is asserted against this by an instrumentation test.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.updates"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths" />
        </provider>
    </application>

</manifest>
